8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Each input has a background, a set of parallel full-length *walls*, and two single-cell *dots* of the same colour.\n- In pairs 1 and 2 the walls are columns; in pair 3 and the test they are rows.\n- Every wall is one colour except for a 3-cell *gate* of another colour (4, 1, 3 and 6 in the four grids).\n\n**What the output adds.** The output draws a path in the dot colour from one dot to the other. I read pair 3 (horizontal walls at rows 3, 8, 17, 23) segment by segment:\n- The path starts at the dot (1,8). It goes down to row 2, the row just before the first wall.\n- On row 2 it runs sideways to column 6, which is the middle of that wall's gate (3,5..7).\n- It goes straight down through the gate centre to row 7, just before the next wall. It slides on row 7 to that wall's gate centre, column 16, and continues the same way.\n- After the last wall it goes down to the end dot's row (27). Then it runs sideways to the end dot (27,12).\n- The gate's middle cell becomes the path colour. The two outer gate cells stay unchanged.\n\nPair 1 is the same idea rotated, with vertical walls at x = 4, 11, 19:\n- From the dot (10,1) the path runs right to x=3 and turns along that column to gate row 4.\n- It crosses to x=10 and turns to row 18, then crosses to x=18 and turns to row 7.\n- It crosses to x=27, the end dot's column, and goes down to the dot at (15,27).\n\nThe fact sheet's segment list agrees, for example \"8 cells right (4,3)->(4,10)\" and \"15 cells down (4,10)->(18,10)\". Pair 2 follows the same pattern.\n\n**The rule.**\n1. Find the wall lines: rows (or columns) that contain no background cell.\n2. If the walls are columns, transpose so they are rows, solve, and transpose back.\n3. The start dot is the one before the first wall. Visit the walls in order of increasing distance from it (step direction d = ±1).\n4. For each wall w, use the gate centre g (the mean position of the minority-colour cells in that wall):\n   - draw vertically from the current point to row w−d;\n   - draw horizontally to column g;\n   - the current point becomes (w−d, g), so the next vertical leg passes through the gate.\n5. Finally, draw vertically to the end dot's row and horizontally to the end dot.\n\n**Applying it to the test.**\n- Walls are rows 3, 8, 19, 22, with gate centres at columns 13, 6, 18, 5.\n- The start dot is (1,4) and the end dot is (28,10).\n- The path is: row 2 to column 13; down to row 7 and over to column 6; down to row 18 and over to column 18; down to row 21 and over to column 5; down to row 28 and over to column 10.\n- The gaps between walls 19 and 22 are tight, but the rule still produces valid segments there.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits one canvas after each wall's approach-and-slide leg, then one after the final leg to the end dot.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g0 = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g0)\n    full_rows = [y for y in range(g0.shape[0]) if np.all(g0[y] != bg)]\n    transposed = False\n    g = g0.copy()\n    if not full_rows:\n        g = g0.T.copy()\n        transposed = True\n        full_rows = [y for y in range(g.shape[0]) if np.all(g[y] != bg)]\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    def show(a):\n        return a.T.copy() if transposed else a.copy()\n\n    # dots: non-background cells outside wall rows\n    dots = [(y, x) for y in range(H) for x in range(W)\n            if y not in full_rows and g[y, x] != bg]\n    dots.sort()\n    col = g[dots[0]]\n    # gates: minority colour in each wall row\n    gates = {}\n    for w in full_rows:\n        vals, cnts = np.unique(g[w], return_counts=True)\n        wallc = vals[np.argmax(cnts)]\n        xs = [x for x in range(W) if g[w, x] != wallc]\n        gates[w] = int(round(sum(xs) / len(xs)))\n    first = min(full_rows)\n    # start dot is on the side of first wall away from others\n    start, end = dots[0], dots[-1]\n    if start[0] > first:\n        start, end = end, start\n    d = 1 if start[0] < end[0] else -1\n    walls = sorted(full_rows, key=lambda w: d * w)\n\n    def vline(x, y0, y1):\n        a, b = sorted((y0, y1))\n        out[a:b + 1, x] = col\n\n    def hline(y, x0, x1):\n        a, b = sorted((x0, x1))\n        out[y, a:b + 1] = col\n\n    cy, cx = start\n    for w in walls:\n        r = w - d\n        vline(cx, cy, r)\n        hline(r, cx, gates[w])\n        cy, cx = r, gates[w]\n        emit(show(out), f\"Path reaches row {r} before wall {w} and slides to its gate centre at {gates[w]}.\")\n    vline(cx, cy, end[0])\n    hline(end[0], cx, end[1])\n    res = show(out)\n    emit(res, \"Path passes the last gate and turns to reach the end dot.\")\n    return res\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pair 1 is the same idea rotated, with vertical walls at x = 4, 11, 19:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"If the walls are columns, transpose so they are rows, solve, and transpose back."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It slides on row 7 to that wall's gate centre, column 16, and continues the same way."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Teaching stages.** The program emits one canvas after each wall's approach-and-slide leg, then one after the final leg to the end dot."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Path reaches row 3 before wall 4 and slides to its gate centre at 4."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output adds.** The output draws a path in the dot colour from one dot to the other."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The path starts at the dot (1,8)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The gate's middle cell becomes the path colour."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"b0518815cd78c5a5cd1100dddfa59d0b24f7ad278147cf320876179083a07de6","task_sha256":"6191f507b5e26b96499459852b0b67edfc3f28976364f3bc4390eab9a2c4b881","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
